1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,266
Same 200 trades. Worst drop: −6% or −23%.

2
00:00:04,267 --> 00:00:08,532
The only difference: how much each trade risked.

3
00:00:08,533 --> 00:00:12,256
We simulated 1,000 traders. Same edge, two sizes.

4
00:00:12,257 --> 00:00:17,066
Risk per trade means what you lose when a stop hits.

5
00:00:17,067 --> 00:00:21,747
In this run, 2% grew more: +117% vs +23%.

6
00:00:21,748 --> 00:00:25,599
It also fell almost four times deeper.

7
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:30,280
All 1,000 runs at 0.5%. Typical worst drop: −6%.

8
00:00:30,281 --> 00:00:34,132
The unluckiest 5% still only fell 11%.

9
00:00:34,133 --> 00:00:37,856
At 2%, the typical worst drop was −23%.

10
00:00:37,857 --> 00:00:42,666
Almost 1 in 5 runs fell over 30% at some point.

11
00:00:42,667 --> 00:00:47,475
Losing streaks: half the runs hit 9 losses in a row.

12
00:00:47,476 --> 00:00:51,199
At 2% each, nine straight losses cost 17%.

13
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:55,844
More risk can grow faster. It also digs deeper pits.

14
00:00:55,845 --> 00:00:59,732
A −30% pit needs +43% to climb out.

15
00:00:59,733 --> 00:01:04,542
You can't pick your losing streaks. You can pick your size.

16
00:01:04,543 --> 00:01:08,266
Size for the drop you can sit through.

17
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:14,616
Lore 006. Next: Lore 007, The Council: where does your stop go?
